# tools/line_cross_witness.rish -- read TWO directed lines, each named by two anchor taps (stroke 1 from A toward B, stroke 2 from C toward D),
# and answer how the two strokes RELATE: do the FINITE strokes cross, do their INFINITE lines cross, are their directions PARALLEL, and where do
# the infinite lines MEET, on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the whole line family until now read ONE directed line over a scene of objects: line_distance how far off it
# a center sits, line_project the exact foot, line_pierce whether the stroke crosses an object's box. line_cross lifts the family from one line and
# its objects to TWO lines and each other -- the read a surface wants the day a keeper draws a second stroke and means "does this cross the one I
# just drew, and if so, exactly where." NO float, NO tolerance, NO square root.
#
# The crux this witness proves on metal: a segment-versus-segment crossing is EXACT with integers (the four ORIENTATIONS split into two
# opposite-sign pairs) and the MEETING POINT of two lines is RATIONAL, held TIMES the direction determinant as an exact i128 pair. Over a 30x26
# scene a HORIZONTAL stroke crosses a VERTICAL stroke at the lattice point (25,21); a vertical stroke stopped short is segments_cross FALSE yet
# lines_cross TRUE (cross_only_infinite); over a 14x14 scene two diagonals cross at (31/3, 47/3), cross_is_lattice FALSE and cross_point_doubled
# refusing CrossNotWhole; parallel strokes refuse Parallel. Reversal invariant; refusals by name; determinism. No network, no key, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/line_cross_witness.rish

say "line_cross: Language EN -- Season G two-stroke crossing; a keeper draws a SECOND stroke and means how it relates to the first, so line_cross reads whether the two FINITE strokes cross, whether their INFINITE lines cross, whether their directions are PARALLEL, and where the lines MEET; the crossing is EXACT by four orientations and the meeting point RATIONAL held exact; NO float, NO tolerance, NO square root."
say "line_cross: Style Radiant -- compose not braid: this rung owns only the two-stroke crossing test over four orientations and the rational meeting point; click_order owns the group a tap lands in, click_collinear owns the anchor centers that name each stroke, line_pierce owns the finite-versus-infinite reading this lifts from one stroke to two. No scene walk, no pixel written -- a reading over readings."
say "line_cross: Lens TAME -- no scene walk at all, only the four fixed anchors of two strokes; the orientation products stay whole in i64 and the scaled meeting point in i128 for any canvas within the image pixel bound; two anchors sharing a group refuse SameGroup, a background tap refuses EmptyClick, a point off the map refuses PointOutOfBounds, parallel strokes refuse Parallel, a fractional meeting asked as a lattice point refuses CrossNotWhole, each having read nothing; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/line_cross"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/line_cross.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "line_cross: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "line_cross: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "line_cross: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-line-cross" else "line_cross: selftest did not print the GREEN line"
assert selftest.out contains "how the two strokes RELATE" else "line_cross: the two-stroke-relation claim did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "lifts the family from one line and its objects to TWO lines and each other" else "line_cross: the lift claim did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "a segment-versus-segment crossing is EXACT with integers" else "line_cross: the exact-crossing crux did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "the MEETING POINT of two lines is RATIONAL" else "line_cross: the rational-meeting crux did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "at the lattice point (25,21) doubled" else "line_cross: the integer-X fact did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "TWO FINITE STROKES CAN MISS WHERE THEIR INFINITE LINES MEET" else "line_cross: the miss crux did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "cross_only_infinite naming exactly that pair" else "line_cross: the cross_only_infinite fact did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "THE MEETING POINT IS RATIONAL, HELD EXACT" else "line_cross: the rational-held-exact crux did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "cross_point_doubled refusing CrossNotWhole rather than rounding a lie" else "line_cross: the CrossNotWhole refusal did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "the scaled point provably lies on BOTH lines" else "line_cross: the on-both-lines identity did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "PARALLEL LINES NEVER MEET AT A POINT" else "line_cross: the parallel crux did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "cross_point_scaled refusing Parallel" else "line_cross: the Parallel refusal did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "refuse SameGroup" else "line_cross: the SameGroup refusal did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "a background tap refuses EmptyClick" else "line_cross: the EmptyClick refusal did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "a point off the map refuses PointOutOfBounds" else "line_cross: the PointOutOfBounds refusal did not print"
assert selftest.out contains "the same two strokes read the same crossing twice" else "line_cross: determinism did not print"

say "line_cross: GREEN on metal -- two directed lines read for how they RELATE: whether the finite strokes cross, whether the infinite lines cross, whether the directions are parallel, and the rational meeting point held exact; two finite strokes missing where their infinite lines meet (cross_only_infinite), the meeting point rational and refused as a lattice point (CrossNotWhole), parallel strokes refusing Parallel; reversal invariant, refusals by name, determinism. Season G open-media."
